# Minimum denominator each gate must report against the dora-loop fixture.
#
# A gate that examines a strictly smaller set while still passing is the
# failure mode no other check here catches. It does not go red, it does not go
# to zero -- the vacuity rule only catches zero -- it just quietly looks at
# less. A coverage analysis found five separate one-line mutations of exactly
# this shape that survived all 21 proofs: scan one commit and report ten,
# analyse one file and count twenty-one, drop the shebang discovery so gradlew
# stops being examined, add -maxdepth 3, count total tests instead of executed
# ones.
#
# So the fixture declares what a gate is expected to see, and the harness
# checks it. These are floors, not exact values: the fixture gains commits and
# tests over time and the numbers only rise. When one legitimately needs
# lowering, that is a visible diff with a reason attached, which is the point.
#
# A gate with no entry here is an error, not a skip. Adding a gate and
# forgetting its floor would otherwise silently opt it out of the one check
# that catches scope reduction.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CATCH. Measured, not assumed -- five mutations were run
# against the full corpus after these floors landed and only one went red:
#
#   caught   secrets narrowing its range to one commit (1 < floor 8), because
#            the denominator now comes from what the scanner reports scanning
#            rather than from a parallel `git rev-list` that never sees the range
#
#   missed   a gate that counts correctly and then hands the tool fewer files.
#            The number stays honest; the work does not. Needs a fault that
#            plants a violation in the LAST file, not a floor.
#   missed   a discovery reduction the fixture is too thin to show. -maxdepth 3
#            changes nothing when the only shell file sits at depth 1. Needs a
#            richer fixture, not a floor.
#   missed   inflation. A floor is a minimum; 9999 passes every one.
#   missed   counting total tests instead of executed ones, because this fixture
#            skips none and the two numbers are identical. Needs the
#            test-all-disabled fault.
#
# So a floor catches shrinkage it can see. Four of those five want faults or a
# better fixture instead, and they are on the roadmap. Saying which is which
# here rather than leaving the impression the floors cover the class.
#
# An entry also declares the gate APPLIES to this fixture. Exit 3 (not
# applicable) then contradicts the declaration and fails -- which is how the
# shebang-discovery mutation gets caught: it made shellcheck declare itself
# irrelevant to a repository it had been scanning, and the harness printed OK.

gradle-wrapper=4
jvm-test=25
# 7, not 8: the runner reported 8 where this host reports 9. gitleaks counts
# commits carrying a diff, and a fresh clone need not agree exactly with a
# long-lived working copy. A floor set at the observed value has no margin and
# becomes a flaky check, which is worse than a loose one.
secrets=7
shellcheck=1
# 20, against 29 measured on a fresh clone of main. Raised from 1, which dated
# from when dora-loop had a README and four ADRs and the docs gate found five
# claims there. It now has wiki sources too. A floor of 1 is not a floor.
docs=20
